speechtext:
Madam Speaker, the hon. member uses the consumer price index to make his comparisons. It is quite obvious, and a study of the consumer price index confirms this, that a short-term analysis based on this index often shows a wide gap between inflation rates in the United States and in Canada. However, if the same figures are applied to a longer period, the gap tends to get narrower. Other measurements might be used and considered by the hon. member. I urge him, for instance, to look at the inflation rate included in the GNE. He will see that for the last four quarters, including the fourth quarter of 1981, the inflation rate in the GNE is 9.6 per cent in Canada and 9.4 per cent in the United States. Of course, the hon. member may reply that you can use whatever figures best prove your case. However, one must take all the figures into account in order to draw logical conclusions.
June 29, 1981

[English}
